The routine scs-a computes the sine and the cosine of a whole number of degrees, from a table every 5 degrees.

Result: 1000 sin x or 1000 cos x rounded to nearest, for x a whole number of degrees from 0 to 359, held
to -999..999. A cell holds three digits, so STO would keep 1000 as 000; the result is capped at 999 instead, which
makes sin 89, 90, 91 and cos 359, 0, 1 all 999, and sin 269, 270, 271 and cos 179, 180, 181 all -999.

Call: load x into the accumulator and JMP 26 for the sine or JMP 22 for the cosine. It returns with the result in
the accumulator.
Cells 22-98 (77 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 58-59, 61, 66-67, 89, 98; nothing else.
The sine: at most 112 instructions a call, 73.5 on average. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (thousandths) against
the correctly rounded value, at x = 1; 88 of the 360 inputs are off.
The cosine: at most 115 instructions a call, 76.8 on average. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (thousandths)
against the correctly rounded value, at x = 3; 88 of the 360 inputs are off.
Costs count from the entry to the return jump; the caller's JMP is not counted.
Address bases: cell 65 holds ADD 69. To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its
instructions that points into cells 22-98 and from the address bases; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are.

